  "explain": {
   "text": "Four pieces are only quarters if they are equal.[^1] A piece is a quarter when four of its size make the whole:[^2] at 250 mm, 1000 ÷ 250 = 4.\n\nSlide to 200 mm. Still one of four pieces, but 1000 ÷ 200 = 5: a fifth. Four copies stop at 800 mm.\n\nThe trap: cut a metre 100, 300, 300, 300 and call two pieces half. You hold 400 or 600 mm, not 500.\n\n**Count how many of the piece fit the whole, not the pieces you see.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "Three phases don't make each a third of the load; equal loads do. With 30 kW in total, a 20 kW phase is no third.",
   "breaks": "A board's total load changes through the day; a cut metre of cable stays put."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** whole ÷ piece. Fits 5 times? It is 1/5.\n\n**Worked example:** a 50 m drum cut into 20, 10, 10 and 10 m runs. 50 ÷ 10 = 5, so each 10 m run is 1/5. No quarters.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Reading the bottom as pieces you can see.\n- Judging equal by eye, not measuring."
  },
